          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What happens if the score is high?",
          "answer": "Which score or test do you mean? It could be a screening questionnaire, a risk score, or a lab value. The meaning of a high result depends on the specific test, so please tell me the name if you know it.\n\nIn general terms, a high result on a screening tool usually means that further assessment is needed. It does not confirm a diagnosis. For example, the guidance on anxiety screening says the seven-item generalized anxiety disorder questionnaire (GAD-7) has commonly used bands of 5 (mild), 10 (moderate) and 15 (severe). A score of 10 is usually the point where further assessment follows (Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care).\n\nRisk scores work differently. They estimate the chance of a future event, and a higher score often leads to a discussion about whether to act, not to an immediate treatment decision. The lipid guidance, for example, describes a risk discussion for borderline and intermediate risk groups (Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation). Any treatment decision depends on the full picture, and a clinician makes it.\n\nLab values vary by test and by laboratory. The retrieved passages do not give result-specific meanings beyond these examples, so I cannot say more without knowing which test you mean.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation]",
